## 1. THE SIMPLE FORM

The whole law is this:

> **If there is no standard, the system is arbitrary.**
>
> **An arbitrary system is not a system.**
>
> **The exception denies the standard.**
>
> **Therefore, the exception is self-negation.**

Everything that follows is commentary on this. The law is not complicated. It follows immediately from what a system is.

A **system** is a set of rules, criteria, or principles that distinguish valid from invalid. The rules are the **standard**. Without the standard, there is no distinction. Without the distinction, there is no system. There is only arbitrariness.

An **exception** is the claim that the standard does not apply in a particular case. But if the standard does not apply, there is no standard. If there is no standard, the system is arbitrary. An arbitrary system is not a system. Therefore, the exception is self-negation.

This is the Universal Law of Standards. It is not a theorem within any particular system. It is the condition of possibility for any system of standards whatsoever—including logic itself.

