## 3. THE PROOF

**Theorem 2:** To make an exception is self-negation.

\[
\mathcal{E}_x \to \neg \mathcal{S} \to \neg \mathcal{F} \to \neg \mathcal{E}_x
\]

where \( \mathcal{E}_x \) denotes the exception.

**Proof:**

1. An exception \( \mathcal{E}_x \) is the claim that the standard \( \mathcal{S} \) does not apply in a particular case.
2. To claim that \( \mathcal{S} \) does not apply is to deny \( \mathcal{S} \).
3. \( \mathcal{S} \) is the condition of possibility of the system \( \mathcal{F} \). To deny \( \mathcal{S} \) is to deny \( \mathcal{F} \).
4. To deny \( \mathcal{F} \) is to deny the framework within which the exception \( \mathcal{E}_x \) was formulated. Therefore, to make an exception is self-negation. ∎

The proof is four steps. It requires no modal logic, no set theory, no apparatus. It follows immediately from what a standard is and what an exception is.

